WEBVTT

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:03.060
MARCO CASALAINA: All right.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.590
Hello, everybody.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.640
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:06.570
I'm Marco Casalaina.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.760
I'm VP Products of CoreAI.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.390
AYCA BAS: Hi, everyone.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.570
I'm Ayca, and I'm a Developer
Advocate at Microsoft.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:12.900
MARCO CASALAINA: All right.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.730
We're going to talk
about the four IQs.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.980
There were three, and
now there are four.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.390
So, Ayca, what is the deal?

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.190
What are the four IQs?

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:23.490
AYCA BAS: Yes.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.000
So seems like IQs are pretty
hot topic this Build.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.270
MARCO CASALAINA: Yeah.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.630
AYCA BAS: And, as you know,
when we build demo agents,

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.050
we can actually use and build
any data source we want.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.710
But, in the real-world, our
data is across the board.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.330
So we need files.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.440
We need structured data,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.110
unstructured data,
human context.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.630
So IQs are actually bringing the
old context data and also M365

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.030
into our agent, which
is pretty cool.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:51.300
MARCO CASALAINA: Yeah.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:51.660
That's right.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:52.890
So there are four IQs.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.460
We got Web IQ, Foundry IQ,
Fabric IQ, and Work IQ.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.530
What do each one of these
things actually do?

00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:58.920
AYCA BAS: Right.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.170
Okay. Let me get
started with Web IQ.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.910
So Web IQ wires your agent
into all that web knowledge

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.070
that actually is pretty useful
because, if you have, let's say,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.430
if you need local information
like the demo you did yesterday,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.700
if you need to check,
let's say, shipment delays

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.920
or all those information,
you kind of need

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.830
that service in place.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:22.440
MARCO CASALAINA: Yeah.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.140
AYCA BAS: So Work IQ is

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.110
where we connect M365
data in your agent.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.230
That means you can
actually check your emails

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:37.230
or Teams messages, all sorts of
data available in Microsoft 365.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.080
And then Fabric IQ, do you
want to cover Fabric IQ?

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:40.440
MARCO CASALAINA: All right.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.810
So Fabric IQ is for
structured data,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.430
largely for structured data.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.250
And you think about you
as humans, you might get

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.220
to this data with a Power BI
report or something like that.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.470
But that doesn't work for an
agent, so you need some kind

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.570
of an agentic face,
a headless way

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:55.920
for these agents to
get to your data.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.560
So Fabric IQ is all about
providing this kind

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.000
of headless access

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.120
and maintaining the context
that's necessary for your agent

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.540
to hit that structured
data in Work IQ.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.380
AYCA BAS: Foundry IQ.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.700
MARCO CASALAINA: Oh.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.690
And Foundry IQ is largely
for unstructured data.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.350
So these are your blob stores
and your search indices,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.420
SharePoint and all
that kind of stuff.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.440
Foundry IQ is an agentic
retrieval mechanism

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.510
that allows your
agents to do that kind

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.950
of agentic retrieval rather

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.850
than simple old-fashioned
rag that's existed

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.930
for like three years.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.060
That makes it old-fashioned
in this market.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.510
Now, Ayca and I pair-programmed
a lot of this stuff.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.760
So we've been working
together for a while,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.310
working towards Build
and stuff like that.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.520
Let's talk a little bit about
so now with the IQs we did,

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.720
and now we got this
on the screen here.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.220
I think you could
see it somewhere;

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.990
or you could put the
PC on the screen.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:49.420
AYCA BAS: I think they can.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.210
MARCO CASALAINA: Production
folks, I hope you can see that.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.210
Now, we've only got
four tools in here.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.350
We got the four IQs.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.440
And, yet, there are still
some idiosyncrasies

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.320
about making your agent call the
right tool at the right time.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.850
How did you -- how
did you do that?

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:03.420
How did we do that?

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:03.690
AYCA BAS: Exactly.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.140
I think we should also explain

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.240
that these services
are very new.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.430
So all those tools
are coming together,

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.000
and Product Teams are working
so hard to bring them together.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.750
So we are in very early
stages of IQs right now.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.710
So, to be able to make them
work in a pretty good shape,

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.100
let's say production ready
shape, then you kind of need

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.010
to maybe work on
your instructions,

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.420
agent instructions.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.610
So we went through
a lot of iterations

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.490
of this agent instructions,
and maybe Marco can cover

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.780
that a little bit too.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.820
But what we have here is very
good and identified descriptions

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.590
for every IQ and how we need
to work together with them,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.260
all of the mapping for,
let's say, Fabric IQ.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.320
And, if I scroll down a little
more, you will see that,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.890
if you're using Work IQ,
there are critical things

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.890
when you're doing search;
when you are doing do action,

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.560
which means if you're doing
any, let's say, sending emails,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.030
etc. We are putting all those
rules so that our agent

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.600
that can actually consume
the IQ content in a shape

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.950
that we want them to be.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:16.780
So we also even have examples of
some JSON buddy that means that,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.300
when Work IQ is
receiving our query,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:26.130
it is pretty much converting our
content into a shape we want to

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.070
and all those stuff
plus everything

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.920
about Web IQ available
in our instructions.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.010
So it actually plays
a really big role.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.610
Having said that, if you just
plug them into an agent and try

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.430
to test, it will
probably work; but,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:45.090
if you want a really good
performance out of the IQs,

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.630
you should definitely consider
adjusting the instructions.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:48.900
MARCO CASALAINA: Right.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.410
So the moral of story is
the purpose of these IQs

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.670
or really one of
the main purposes

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.510
of these IQs is
preventing context rot.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:00.290
It's removing so you don't have
to put all of this context stuff

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:03.290
in each individual agent that
you build, all the context

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.350
about your data and about your
unstructured data source,

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.820
about Microsoft ecosystem stuff.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.700
But that does not absolve
you from still having

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.690
to put some context in here
to tell it what tool to call

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.540
when and what they do.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.170
That is still on you or on
whatever coding agent you happen

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:22.400
to be using because, yes;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.730
we absolutely did do
this with Copilot CLI.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:29.780
Now, I think, to finish this
off, what is really interesting

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.810
about this, so here she
has the Foundry agent.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.090
That's what it looks like
in the Foundry web portal.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.820
But that is not how
we expose this agent.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.170
How we exposed this agent is
fascinating and, honestly,

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.990
was a little bit
undersold here at Build

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.520
in the keynote and
stuff like that.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.310
So, if you would, production
folks, if you can hear me,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.660
please change to the Mac.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.400
I'm on a Mac; she's on a PC.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:54.390
So here we go.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.830
So check this out.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.070
So we did indeed expose
that same agent,

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.020
but what did we do with it?

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.640
We did this thing.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.090
Out of that agent, we
made an agent template.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.450
So this is an agent
template in A 365.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.680
And what does that give you?

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.360
When you have an agent
template, what it does is this:

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.580
It allows you, or
really anybody,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.400
to create an instance of this.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.040
When you create an
instance of this agent,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.350
you're not just
creating an agent.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.050
So, when I created an
instance of this thing,

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.730
I didn't just make an agent.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.130
I made my agent.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:36.480
This is my instance of
this particular refund

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.680
processor agent.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.270
That means you see it in the org
chart, and it reports to me.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:47.070
That also means that it has
its own Teams box over here

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:51.420
and that it has its own inbox.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.080
It has its own email address
and all that kind of stuff.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:59.550
And so earlier I told it, check
your email for complaints,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.950
and I mean your email.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.630
To the agent, I'm like,
Check your email,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.700
not my email, your email.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.320
It has its own.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.210
And it's able to
check its own email.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.180
And, in fact, it sent me back,
if I look at my own inbox

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:16.140
in here, you can see that
it sent to Maria Garcia,

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.530
it sent a reply;
and it copied me.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.630
Now, this is hugely
advantageous.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.980
Not all agents are
going to be like this.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.370
But there are certain class
of agents that you want

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.710
to have their own
identity like this,

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:31.830
and there is specifically
for security.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:35.010
For one thing, if we think
about, say I go over here

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.590
to ClawPilot, which keeps
signing me out today.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.390
So here I am in ClawPilot.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.800
Now, for those of you who
haven't seen this before,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.810
this is our kind of
internal claw thing,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.120
and it uses the heck
out of Work IQ.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.970
Here earlier today I was making
it read a document for me,

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.680
and it used these tools.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.210
It used Work IQ.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:54.150
It used this to draft an
email and all this stuff.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:58.260
But it was doing this
on behalf of me,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:03.540
so that means this agent could
do anything that I can do.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.820
However, that's not
always wonderful.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.700
Wow. What is my mouse doing?

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.190
That is not always what
you want to be doing here.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.130
So, in this case,
this agent runs

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:15.930
in its own security context.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:17.670
It has its own permissions.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.040
It can't just do
everything that I can do.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.870
So I might say,
all right, agent.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:25.890
You have the ability to send
emails, to send Teams messages

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.630
and stuff; but you
are not allowed

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.580
to delete stuff out of OneDrive.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.340
I can; agent cannot.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.560
So it has its own
security posture.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.620
And so, as you start to think
about deploying these agents

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.150
to an organization,
you do need to think

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.640
about what is your
security posture.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.920
And the on behalf of
security posture,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.620
like what I was showing earlier,
is not always what you want.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.000
What you do want, in many cases,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.260
is an agent that has its own
identity, its own security.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.740
AYCA BAS: Awesome, And if
you switch to my PC --

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:56.460
MARCO CASALAINA: Back to the PC.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.500
AYCA BAS: -- I want to show,
and before we close this

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.470
out I want to show that, while
Marco was going through all

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:05.070
that chat with A 365 agent,
you can also see all

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.130
that in the traces of it
called between the tools,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.890
between Work IQ and
then Fabric IQ;

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.770
and then Foundry IQ
returned saying that, Hey.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.530
Maria Garcia, I
found her package.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.330
And then I checked the policies,

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:20.880
and then I can actually
issue her a refund.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.830
So I can email to Maria.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.550
So all that is happening in sync
behind the scene calling all the

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.290
IQs in synchronously.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:31.740
So this is pretty much what
we wanted to talk about.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.170
But there is one thing
I want to mention.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.430
MARCO CASALAINA: All right.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.740
Of course, this is
just 13 minutes talk.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.170
But, if you want to
learn more about IQs,

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.640
we have a series for you.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.070
Just go ahead and check
out the IQ series.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.090
It's ak.ms/iq-series.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.660
We have episodes for you
about Foundry IQ, Work IQ;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.300
and Fabric IQ and Web
IQ are coming soon.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.980
And we also issue you a digital
badge if you complete cookbooks

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.480
and if you watch
all the episodes.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.730
So you can show the
world that you're one

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.400
of the first experts about IQs.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:06.660
Yeah. So that's pretty much it.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:07.740
You want to close it out.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:08.190
I will.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:09.360
Thank you, Ayca.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.570
And thank you all, and
use yourself some IQs.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.520
Have a good day.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.500
